<p>Most advice about becoming an influencer begins at the wrong end. It starts with followers, hooks, cameras, posting schedules, or whatever platform happens to be loudest this month. Those things matter, but they are not the work. The work is becoming someone whose attention is worth borrowing.</p>
<p>That requires more than visibility. It requires a point of view, a repeatable practice, and enough regard for your audience that you refuse to treat them as a number that can be traded. An audience can feel the difference between a person trying to be noticed and a person trying to be useful. The second person has a much better chance of lasting.</p>
<h2>Start with a point of view, not a persona</h2>
<p>An influencer is not simply a person who appears online. It is a person whose judgment helps other people make sense of something. Your subject might be books, beauty, finance, cooking, fitness, technology, or a small corner of culture. The question is not, “What kind of person should I pretend to be?” It is, “What do I see clearly enough to help someone else see?”</p>
<p>A useful point of view sits at the intersection of three things: the questions you keep returning to, the experience you have earned, and the people you can genuinely help. Patrick’s own work on <a href="/books/boosting-the-size-of-sale">influence and persuasion</a> begins with that harder question: why do certain voices become trusted in the first place?</p>
<p>Make a short list of the subjects you can stay curious about after the first burst of enthusiasm fades. Then make the list narrower. “Technology” is a territory. “How new technology changes who gets to lead” is a perspective. “Books” is a territory. “Books for readers who want to think more slowly about the present” is a perspective. You do not need a slogan. You need a lens.</p>
<picture><source srcset="/images/blog/find-your-point-of-view-7008ffe377169251.webp" type="image/webp"/><img src="/images/blog/find-your-point-of-view-96924a0ad95d39ba.jpg" alt="Notes and books arranged around a hand-drawn idea map" loading="lazy" decoding="async"/></picture>
<p>Clarity is more attractive than breadth at the beginning. People follow when they can tell what you notice, what you care about, and what kind of help they will receive. Your work can expand later. For now, make it easy for the right people to recognize themselves in the room.</p>
<h2>Choose one useful format and one primary home</h2>
<p>You do not have to be everywhere. In fact, trying to be everywhere before you know what you are making is one of the quickest ways to produce a lot of noise and learn very little. Pick one primary platform based on the format that makes your thinking strongest. If you explain through voice, try audio or video. If you need room to develop an argument, write. If your eye is your strength, work visually. The medium should serve the idea, not flatter your anxiety about being absent.</p>
<p>Use secondary channels as signposts, not as a second full-time job. A short video can lead to a longer essay. A newsletter can point to a book, a conversation, or a more useful archive. The goal is not to trap people inside one app. It is to give them a reliable way to return to your work. That is why a home you control, whether it is a website, a mailing list, or a growing library of work, matters.</p>
<p>When you do choose a platform, learn its rules before you lean on it for income. The <a href="https://support.google.com/youtube/answer/72851?hl=en" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">YouTube Partner Program overview</a> explains current eligibility and monetization requirements. Instagram also publishes <a href="https://creators.instagram.com/best-practices/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">creator best practices</a> for people building on its platform. Reading the rules yourself is better than building a plan around a clip of somebody else explaining them.</p>
<h2>Build a practice people can recognize</h2>
<p>Consistency is often mistaken for frequency. Posting every day can be useful if it helps you practice, but it is not a virtue by itself. The better question is whether your audience can recognize the standard you are trying to uphold. A weekly essay, a thoughtful video every other week, or three useful posts a month can build more trust than a relentless stream of forgettable material.</p>
<p>Give yourself a small system. Keep a running file of questions from readers. Save examples that complicate your point of view. Decide which ideas deserve a quick response and which need time. Create recurring formats that reduce the pressure to invent your entire identity from scratch every morning. A useful rhythm might include one observation, one explanation, one recommendation, and one invitation for conversation.</p>
<picture><source srcset="/images/blog/build-a-repeatable-practice-44a39f3d6f2a6057.webp" type="image/webp"/><img src="/images/blog/build-a-repeatable-practice-6b995c7d12d4dd6a.jpg" alt="Content planning calendar, notebooks, and timer on a sunny desk" loading="lazy" decoding="async"/></picture>
<p>Protect some of your time from immediate reaction. The internet rewards speed, but speed has a habit of making everyone sound the same. Depth is slower to produce, which is exactly why it can become memorable. The ideas in <a href="/books/iq-an-overrated-quotient">IQ: An Overrated Quotient</a> are a useful reminder that making something worthwhile usually asks for both creative instinct and technical discipline.</p>
<h2>Make work that gives before it asks</h2>
<p>Early influence is an exchange of attention. Someone gives you a few seconds, a few minutes, or an hour. Your responsibility is to return something worthwhile: a clearer question, a practical next step, a well-chosen example, a useful warning, a source they can check, or language for a problem they could not quite name.</p>
<p>This does not mean every post must be a tutorial. A well-made opinion can be useful. A personal story can be useful. A book recommendation can be useful. The test is simple: after engaging with your work, does a person have more than they had before? If the answer is consistently yes, the relationship begins to deepen.</p>
<p>Be especially careful with claims that affect someone’s money, health, safety, or reputation. Explain what you know, what you do not know, and where the limits are. That restraint is not a weakness. It is part of the reason people will believe you when you have something important to say. When you use another person’s work, do not treat a repost as an entitlement either. The U.S. Copyright Office’s overview of <a href="https://www.copyright.gov/fair-use/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">fair use</a> is a useful starting point for understanding why context matters.</p>
<h2>Talk with people, not at an audience</h2>
<p>Followers are not an audience until there is some exchange. Reply when there is a real question. Notice patterns in the comments and messages. Ask what your readers are trying to solve. Pay attention to the language they use, because it will often be clearer than the language you were planning to use.</p>
<p>You do not need to be constantly available. Boundaries make a practice sustainable. But a creator who only broadcasts eventually loses touch with the people the work is meant to serve. A small, thoughtful community can teach you more than a dashboard full of anonymous reach.</p>
<p>It also keeps your public voice human. <a href="/books/ceos-of-tiktok">CEOs of TikTok</a> explores how technology has changed who gets to lead and who gets heard. The opportunity is real, but so is the responsibility: access to attention is not a license to become careless with it.</p>
<h2>Earn trust before you try to monetize it</h2>
<p>There is nothing shameful about making money from your work. Money can buy the time required to make better work. But monetization works best when it follows a clear promise. A book, a workshop, a service, a partnership, a membership, or a product should feel like a natural next step for someone who already understands what you are about.</p>
<p>Before accepting a partnership, ask three questions. Would I recommend this if I were not paid? Can I explain why it fits my audience? Would I be comfortable discussing the relationship in plain language? If any answer is no, the short-term fee may cost more trust than it earns.</p>
<picture><source srcset="/images/blog/earn-trust-before-income-2d701a1f85ac8c79.webp" type="image/webp"/><img src="/images/blog/earn-trust-before-income-70d87f82821fdcfe.jpg" alt="Two people exchanging a handwritten note across a wooden table" loading="lazy" decoding="async"/></picture>
<p>In the United States, paid relationships and free products connected to an endorsement should be made clear to the audience. The Federal Trade Commission’s <a href="https://www.ftc.gov/business-guidance/resources/disclosures-101-social-media-influencers" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Disclosures 101 for social media influencers</a> is a practical place to start, alongside its plain-language guidance on <a href="https://www.ftc.gov/business-guidance/resources/ftcs-endorsement-guides-what-people-are-asking" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">endorsement questions</a>. A disclosure should be easy to notice and easy to understand, not hidden behind a maze of tags.</p>
<p>Trust grows slowly, then becomes an asset. It is why a smaller group of readers who value your recommendations can support more meaningful work than a huge group that only watches you pass by.</p>
<h2>Measure the right signs of progress</h2>
<p>Follower count is visible, which makes it seductive. It is not useless, but it is incomplete. Look for signals that your work is landing: people saving it, replying with specific questions, returning to read more, sharing it with a reason, subscribing, buying something that genuinely helps them, or telling you that an idea changed what they did next.</p>
<p>Keep a simple monthly record. What did you publish? What did people return to? Which questions kept appearing? What felt alive enough to develop further? This turns growth into a practice of observation instead of a daily verdict on your worth.</p>
<p>Some growth will be slow, and that is fine. A library of useful work compounds differently from a burst of attention. The best creators do not merely chase reach. They become easier to return to.</p>
<h2>Keep your voice when the pressure rises</h2>
<p>As your audience grows, you will receive advice to become sharper, louder, more certain, more available, and more marketable. Some advice will be helpful. Some of it will turn you into a copy of the thing people already have too much of. Your voice is not a fixed personality trait. It is the set of choices you keep making about what deserves attention and how you will treat the people who give you theirs.</p>
<p>Make room for revision. Say when you changed your mind. Decline work that would make your recommendations feel rented. Take breaks before resentment turns into contempt for the people you are trying to serve. Those choices protect the relationship that makes influence possible.</p>
<h2>A better way to begin</h2>
<p>Start smaller than your ambition. Choose one question you can answer well. Make one useful thing for one specific kind of person. Return next week with a clearer version. Over time, your audience will not simply see more content. They will see a body of work taking shape.</p>
<p>For readers thinking about influence as a craft rather than a shortcut, <a href="/books/boosting-the-size-of-sale">Boosting the Size of Sale</a> offers a deeper look at what makes a message trusted and a voice worth listening to. You can also <a href="/books">browse Patrick’s full shelf</a> for books on technology, identity, creativity, and the work of making something last.</p>
<p>And if you are building something you want to talk through, <a href="/contact">write to Patrick</a>. The most durable work often begins with a better question, not a better trick.</p>
<section class="article-faq"><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
<div><h3>How many followers do you need to become an influencer?</h3>
<p>There is no useful universal number. A small audience that trusts your judgment, returns for your work, and acts on your recommendations is more valuable than a large audience that barely notices you. Start by making something consistently useful for a specific group of people.</p>
</div>
<div><h3>Do you need to choose one social platform?</h3>
<p>No, but you do need a primary home while you learn. Choose the place where your natural format makes sense, then use other channels to point people back to your best work. Spreading the same unfinished idea everywhere usually makes it harder to improve.</p>
</div>
<div><h3>Can you make money before you have a large audience?</h3>
<p>Yes. Readers and viewers can support a useful body of work through services, products, consulting, speaking, memberships, affiliate relationships, or books. The sensible order is trust first, then an offer that fits the trust you have earned.</p>
</div>
<div><h3>What makes an influencer credible?</h3>
<p>Credibility comes from a clear point of view, work that shows care, honest limits, and recommendations that remain useful after the post is over. Disclose paid relationships plainly and decline opportunities that would confuse the relationship with your audience.</p>
</div>
</section>
